    <h3 id="t-circle-residue">Why it belongs in the residue essay</h3>
    <p>Without deciding the supernatural question, the archive displays the complete cultural path that this
      essay calls distribution: an experience is attributed to a source, filtered through an instrument and a
      group, put into language, edited into a durable record, and encountered again by people elsewhere and
      later. A more honest receiving equation therefore makes the claimed source only one term:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      T_{i,s}=\mathcal E_s\!\left(
      \mathcal K_i[R?]+H_i+C_s+G_s+\varepsilon_{i,s}
      \right),
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(T_{i,s}\) is the surviving transcript from instrument \(i\) in session \(s\),
      \(\mathcal K_i[R?]\) is the <em>hypothesised</em> reception of a residue or contact,
      \(H_i\) is the instrument&rsquo;s memory and vocabulary, \(C_s\) the immediate setting,
      \(G_s\) the circle&rsquo;s shared expectations and interaction, and \(\mathcal E_s\) the later processes of
      recording, transcription and editing. The question mark is essential. A meaningful or internally coherent
      text cannot determine which term produced which sentence.</p>
    <p>On the historical reading, the Circle is a trace-making institution: people inherit L/L Research&rsquo;s
      concepts, reinterpret them together and distribute new recordings and texts. On the structural reading,
      its recurring concern with the One and the many exemplifies a human philosophical problem. Only on the
      strongest metaphysical reading might the sessions be instances of nonlocal reception &mdash; and the
      archive alone cannot promote that possibility into fact. Its value here is as a transparent example of
      how a claimed transmission becomes a social, linguistic and durable residue.</p>

